      "problem": "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{22}-{x}^{20}+3x-2\\,$, find $f(A).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+8 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"left\">$\\,\\,{x}^{20}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">${x}^{2}-1\\,$</td>\n<td colspan=\"7\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"left\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{22}\\,-\\,{x}^{20}\\,+\\,3x\\,-\\,2\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">${x}^{22}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">${x}^{20}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$3x$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(3x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{20}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =3A-2I \\\\  & =3[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
